 B-minor downbeat.","quarter_range":[0,4]},{"start_bar":57,"end_bar":60,"why":"Alternating Bm/F# and Em7/G, with an exact two-bar melodic restatement; audition the bass seam.","quarter_range":[112,120]}],"hook_candidates":[{"start_bar":3,"end_bar":6,"why":"The late F#–C#–D–C# tune against a falling bass."},{"start_bar":61,"end_bar":64,"why":"Octave melody over a descending bass, suited to a larger refrain."}],"kpop_uses":[{"where":"verse","why":"Two-bar opening bed leaves space for voice; retain the off-beat melody as an answer."},{"where":"pre-chorus","why":"Bars 19–26 gradually widen into octaves; use that expansion to prepare the chorus."},{"where":"bridge","why":"Bars 31–40 expose unaccompanied cascades for a vocal or instrumental reset."},{"where":"chorus","why":"Bars 57–64 give a returning tonic theme with octave growth. This is a related-key return, not a step-up final-chorus lift."}],"review_flags":[{"bar":11,"reason":"Functional pivot reading is interpretive: E#dim7 → F#m is note-supported but the previous B-minor context is chromatically extended."},{"bar":41,"reason":"Key-area boundaries at 41/45/49/57 describe local theme regions; distinguish sequence and tonicization from large-scale modulation by ear."}],"summary":"A B-minor miniature with a triplet engine and a late, off-beat tune. Its strongest turn is a related-key F#-minor restatement; the later whole-tone descent through A minor and G major is more unsettled. The final return is a story arrival, followed by a chromatic coda rather than a single uninterrupted cadence.","tricks":["Bars 3–10: a descending chromatic/stepwise bass under a slower song; the triplets make the two layers breathe differently.","Bars 11–18: the earlier idea shifts to F# minor, including its own applied dominants.","Bars 41–56: related phrase models descend through B minor, A minor and G major.","Bars 69 and 73–75: C/E (Neapolitan colour) contrasts with an explicit F#7b9 and a tonic return."],"schema_version":1,"bars":[{"bar"